1. whoami: Displays current username
2. hostname: Displays system hostname
3. pwd (present working directory): Displays the current working directory.

4. ls: List all the files and directories.
      ls -l : Displays detailed file information.
      ls -lt: Lists files by modification time.
      ls -a : Displays hidden files.
      ls -lr: list files and directories in reverse order.
5. mkdir : Creates directory
6. mkdir -p : Creates nested directories
7. rmdir: Removes empty directory
8. cd:   Changes directory 	        
9. cd .. : Moves to parent directory     
10 tree: Displays directory structure

11. touch: Creates an empty file
12. cat: Displays file content
13. more: Views file content page by page
14. less: Views file page by page
15. head: Displays first 10 lines
16. tail: Displays last 10 lines
17. cp:   Copies files
18. mv: Moves or renames files
19. rm: Removes files
20. rm -r: Removes directories recursively
21. file: Identifies file type

22. echo: Prints or writes text
23. grep: Searches for text/ Pattern
24. find: Searches for files
25. wc -l: Counts lines
26. wc -w: Counts words
27. wc -m: Counts the characters
28. wc -c: Counts bytes
29. sort: Sorts lines of a file
30. uniq: Removes adjacent duplicate lines
31. cut: Extracts columns or fields
32. awk: Processes text and columns
33. sed: Searches and replaces text

34. who: Shows logged-in users	
35. id: Displays user and group IDs
36. adduser: Creates a user interactively (Ubuntu)
37. passwd: Changes user password
38. usermod:  Modifies user account
39. groupadd: Creates a group
40. groups: Displays group membership
41. userdel: Deletes a user
42. groupdel: Deletes a group
